one part can change how the whole system behaves.","Battery, lamp, switch/wires; lamp stays off if switch open.","Careful use of part with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising system?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named part, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the part used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded."],"question":"A learner is practising system. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence.","options":["Decide from which object looks most exciting.","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation.","Listing parts without saying how they connect misses the system idea.","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone."],"question":"New examples: a charging phone, a nesting bird, glass marble, fallen dry leaf. Which habit makes a scientific sort or claim trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim.","options":["Named observation plus how it supports the claim.","A longer story with no observation.","A label copied from the title with no evidence.","Listing parts without saying how they connect misses the system idea."],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about system?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Battery, lamp, switch/wires; lamp stays off if switch open. applies the science idea with evidence.","options":["Listing parts without saying how they connect misses the system idea.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Battery, lamp, switch/wires; lamp stays off if switch open."],"question":"Using this idea — A system has parts that work together; changing one part can change how the whole system behaves. — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable and control the rest.","options":["Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer.","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same.","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster.","Stop early when you like the first result."],"question":"Which investigation habit keeps a comparison fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer needs a new object or context with evidence.","options":["Repeat the worked example objects unchanged.","Invent a dramatic result with no observation.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Test or describe a new object or context and cite fresh evidence."],"question":"Which next step best shows transfer of system?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses system. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Battery, lamp, switch/wires; lamp stays off if switch open. Reasoning: I used system by attending to part rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason.","question":"Name one science misconception about system, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Listing parts without saying how they connect misses the system idea. Correction: Battery, lamp, switch/wires; lamp stays off if switch open. Why: the correction restores accurate use of system.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y4-w28-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise system.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Engineering and Design","success_criteria":["I can use system with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify part and explain why it matters.","I can practise together independently on a new prompt.","I can notice input needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A system has parts that work together; changing one part can change how the whole system behaves. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","A system has parts that work together; changing one part can change how the whole system behaves.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about system?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBicycle: pedals, chain, wheels. Remove the chain and pedalling no longer drives the wheel. Input (pedal turn) → parts → output (move).\n\nTRY FIRST\nName three parts of a torch system and what happens if the switch part fails open.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBattery, lamp, switch/wires; lamp stays off if switch open.","explain":"A system has parts that work together; changing one part can change how the whole system behaves.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare 