e wheel. Input (pedal turn) → parts →  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about system?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBicycle: pedals, chain, wheels. Remove the chain and pedalling no longer drives the wheel. Input (pedal turn) → parts → output (move). Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct th