 claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Listing parts without saying how they connect misses the system idea. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about system?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA system has parts that work together; changing one part can change how the whole system behaves. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 28, Lesson 1: Systems and how parts work together","unit_number":6,"unit_title":"Mechanisms and systems","week":28,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nBicycle: pedals, chain, wheels. Remove the chain and pedalling no longer drives the wheel. Input (pedal turn) → parts → output (move).\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Systems thinking.","caption":"Input → parts → output.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">System</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">parts together</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Fail one part</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">output changes</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Failure predictions.","caption":"Two systems.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" widt